     1,
     "\r<br>Note that the value of &#39;E&#39; is not part of the fitted.values (its a part of the\r<br>likelihood...), but you can move &#39;E&#39; into the linear predictor using argument\r<br>\r<br>offset = log(expected_hosp)\r<br>\r<br>instead of\r<br>\r<br>E = expected_hosp\r<br>\r<br>or add offset(log(expected_hosp)) to the formula\r<br>\r<br>&gt; y=10\r<br>&gt; r=inla(y~1,data=data.frame(y), family=&quot;poisson&quot;)\r<br>&gt; r$summary.fitted.values$mean\r<br>[1] 9.999991924\r<br>&gt; r=inla(y~1,E=10, data=data.frame(y), family=&quot;poisson&quot;)\r<br>&gt; r$summary.fitted.values$mean\r<br>[1] 0.9999991925\r<br>&gt; r=inla(y~1,offset=log(10), data=data.frame(y), family=&quot;poisson&quot;)\r<br>&gt; r$summary.fitted.values$mean\r<br>[1] 9.99998304\r<br>&gt; r=inla(y~1 + offset(log(10)), data=data.frame(y), family=&quot;poisson&quot;)\r<br>&gt; r$summary.fitted.values$mean\r<br>[1] 9.99998304\r<br>"
